1
00:00:07,439 --> 00:00:14,727
[SPEAKER_00]: I was thinking maybe you have something in mind, since you don't get to come here often.

2
00:00:14,848 --> 00:00:15,929
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll be listening.

3
00:00:17,531 --> 00:00:19,173
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless you want to just hear me ask.

4
00:00:19,633 --> 00:00:22,036
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that would be alright.

5
00:00:22,076 --> 00:00:23,458
[SPEAKER_01]: He's been asking the last two days.

6
00:00:26,244 --> 00:00:36,074
[SPEAKER_00]: So, one thing I was just curious about, how your interpretation of a certain sutta, this is from the Sanyuktinikāya, second Khandhasamyukta.

7
00:00:36,794 --> 00:00:41,679
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the one where the Buddha compares form to a lump of foam, feelings to bubble.

8
00:00:42,540 --> 00:00:43,220
[SPEAKER_00]: Remember that one?

9
00:00:43,381 --> 00:00:43,641
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

10
00:00:45,463 --> 00:00:45,723
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

11
00:00:46,362 --> 00:00:53,603
[SPEAKER_00]: For example, one of the stanzas says, bhikkhu, suppose this river Ganges was carrying a great long, great lump of foam.

12
00:00:54,283 --> 00:00:58,644
[SPEAKER_00]: A man with good sight would inspect it, ponder it, and carefully investigate it.

13
00:00:59,024 --> 00:01:02,205
[SPEAKER_00]: And it would appear to him to be void, hollow, and substantial.

14
00:01:02,885 --> 00:01:05,265
[SPEAKER_00]: For what substance could there be in a lump of foam?

15
00:01:06,045 --> 00:01:15,907
[SPEAKER_00]: So too, bhikkhus, whatever kind of form there is, whether past, future, or present, internal or external, gross or subtle, inferior or superior, far or near,

16
00:01:16,498 --> 00:01:23,461
[SPEAKER_00]: Bhikkhu inspects it, ponders it, and carefully investigates it, and it would appear to him to be void, hollow, or insubstantial.

17
00:01:23,922 --> 00:01:26,343
[SPEAKER_00]: For what substance could there be in form?

18
00:01:28,444 --> 00:01:30,525
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, this is just a translation.

19
00:01:30,765 --> 00:01:36,788
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know off the top of my head what the exact Pali word is for void, hollow, insubstantial.

20
00:01:36,808 --> 00:01:37,308
[SPEAKER_00]: Inspect.

21
00:01:38,549 --> 00:01:39,569
[SPEAKER_00]: Inspect, as well.

22
00:01:40,010 --> 00:01:40,430
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

23
00:01:40,670 --> 00:01:41,951
[SPEAKER_01]: The Pali word is for that.

24
00:01:43,065 --> 00:01:45,507
[SPEAKER_00]: I can, I can look up to Pali, if you'd like me to.

25
00:01:45,527 --> 00:01:46,207
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's okay.

26
00:01:46,307 --> 00:01:46,948
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

27
00:01:48,289 --> 00:02:00,937
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I don't know if it's just a translation, but to me, it makes it sound like that kind of, you know, emptiness form, form is emptiness kind of thing, where form doesn't actually exist.

28
00:02:01,077 --> 00:02:02,498
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just a lump, like a foam.

29
00:02:02,518 --> 00:02:03,619
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no center to it.

30
00:02:04,359 --> 00:02:04,699
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no.

31
00:02:04,719 --> 00:02:05,400
[SPEAKER_01]: See, that's the thing.

32
00:02:06,361 --> 00:02:07,641
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody says it doesn't exist.

33
00:02:08,122 --> 00:02:11,364
[SPEAKER_01]: It just means it's hollow and void in its existence.

34
00:02:13,228 --> 00:02:16,549
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't mean you denial the actual appearance of the bubble, of the foam.

35
00:02:18,049 --> 00:02:27,471
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you recognize the nature of that which is, well, very, very transient, so to speak, without any, any everlasting substance.

36
00:02:28,072 --> 00:02:30,972
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you recognize the same characteristics in this very foam.

37
00:02:32,133 --> 00:02:37,434
[SPEAKER_01]: But that doesn't, that doesn't mean that, that the whole thing does not exist.

38
00:02:37,934 --> 00:02:41,875
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is, that's exactly like what you, what you have

39
00:02:42,709 --> 00:02:45,670
[SPEAKER_01]: What you have just said, the emptiness form is emptiness.

40
00:02:46,931 --> 00:02:52,194
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, that's true, in a general sense, but now, what would a person mean by that?

41
00:02:52,294 --> 00:02:55,435
[SPEAKER_01]: And often, they mean denial, denial of things.

42
00:02:55,935 --> 00:02:57,476
[SPEAKER_01]: Emptiness, that means it's not real, it's not there.

43
00:02:57,496 --> 00:02:59,077
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it is there, as empty.

44
00:03:00,178 --> 00:03:02,599
[SPEAKER_01]: If there's nothing there, you can't distinguish even emptiness.

45
00:03:04,039 --> 00:03:10,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if nothing is there, there's absolutely no reference point for full or empty, the substance or no substance.

46
00:03:10,262 --> 00:03:11,163
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it is very real,

47
00:03:12,038 --> 00:03:13,059
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's fleeting.

48
00:03:13,239 --> 00:03:18,363
[SPEAKER_01]: It's non-substantial, so to speak.

49
00:03:18,883 --> 00:03:20,164
[SPEAKER_01]: But as such, it is real.

50
00:03:21,325 --> 00:03:26,529
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why you recognize that and then you apply the same to your form, to the form that pertains to the sense of self.

51
00:03:26,549 --> 00:03:26,769
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

52
00:03:26,809 --> 00:03:37,738
[SPEAKER_01]: Because sense of self cannot be established upon that which is fleeting and non-substantial and void and hollow and transient.

53
00:03:38,855 --> 00:03:48,081
[SPEAKER_01]: So, applying that to that upon which your sense of self depends, undermines the sense of self, but leaves the bubbles of the foam intact.

54
00:03:48,781 --> 00:03:49,642
[SPEAKER_01]: They were always that.

55
00:03:50,702 --> 00:03:52,904
[SPEAKER_01]: What has changed is your attitude in regard to them.

56
00:03:54,324 --> 00:04:05,131
[SPEAKER_01]: Appropriation, they used to exist upon the assumption that there is substance to it, there is the actual ground for the self.

57
00:04:05,711 --> 00:04:06,392
[SPEAKER_01]: That has changed.

58
00:04:06,432 --> 00:04:08,313
[SPEAKER_01]: That's gone and then there's appropriation constant.

59
00:04:09,938 --> 00:04:13,000
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the same thing applies then for the rest of the similes, I suppose.

60
00:04:13,400 --> 00:04:15,061
[SPEAKER_00]: The perceptions like an illusion.

61
00:04:15,081 --> 00:04:16,462
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, for all the aggregates, yeah.

62
00:04:16,942 --> 00:04:18,463
[SPEAKER_00]: Inventions like a plantain.

63
00:04:18,783 --> 00:04:20,764
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, see that whole thing like, oh, it's an illusion.

64
00:04:21,205 --> 00:04:22,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it is.

65
00:04:22,105 --> 00:04:23,566
[SPEAKER_01]: You can regard it, it's an illusion.

66
00:04:23,666 --> 00:04:26,528
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, that doesn't mean that it's not real as such.

67
00:04:27,908 --> 00:04:29,890
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody in the society doesn't say it does not exist.

68
00:04:30,150 --> 00:04:31,631
[SPEAKER_01]: It's an illusion and it does not exist.

69
00:04:32,231 --> 00:04:36,433
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, when you say illusion, and maybe that's because of all of the other

70
00:04:37,995 --> 00:04:46,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Schools of Buddhism or other religious practices, people nowadays, you say illusion, that means like, oh, it's not real.

71
00:04:47,664 --> 00:04:48,645
[SPEAKER_01]: But illusion is real.

72
00:04:49,926 --> 00:04:52,208
[SPEAKER_01]: If it weren't real, you wouldn't be ignorant in the first place.

73
00:04:53,510 --> 00:04:54,731
[SPEAKER_01]: It's real as such.

74
00:04:55,051 --> 00:04:58,374
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically illusion means it's different to what you think it is.

75
00:04:59,696 --> 00:05:00,897
[SPEAKER_01]: So you're deluded by it.

76
00:05:01,958 --> 00:05:03,199
[SPEAKER_01]: But when that changes, that

77
00:05:03,888 --> 00:05:07,689
[SPEAKER_01]: Which, you were deluded by in the beginning, still remains.

78
00:05:07,789 --> 00:05:10,530
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, what has changed is your delusion about it.

79
00:05:10,550 --> 00:05:11,051
[SPEAKER_01]: That's gone.

80
00:05:12,411 --> 00:05:13,412
[SPEAKER_01]: But the thing remains.

81
00:05:14,112 --> 00:05:16,373
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, the essay, dharmata-sacca.

82
00:05:16,833 --> 00:05:19,034
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want to do that, right?

83
00:05:19,054 --> 00:05:19,914
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want to be resisted.

84
00:05:19,934 --> 00:05:21,054
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to be resisted.

85
00:05:21,074 --> 00:05:21,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, yes, yes.

86
00:05:21,515 --> 00:05:23,555
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not the actual dharmata-sacca.

87
00:05:24,216 --> 00:05:30,678
[SPEAKER_00]: The last one, which I don't know if I have always thought too far into it, but even this one,

88
00:05:31,324 --> 00:05:37,106
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, there are works that go very deep into detail into it, whether they're right or wrong.

89
00:05:37,806 --> 00:05:41,247
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you know, those Magic of the Mind, I think.

90
00:05:41,347 --> 00:05:45,188
[SPEAKER_00]: One of his books, where he compares consciousness to a magician's show.

91
00:05:45,769 --> 00:05:51,390
[SPEAKER_00]: It's saying, suppose that a magician or a magician's apprentice would display a magical illusion at the crossroads.

92
00:05:51,991 --> 00:05:54,891
[SPEAKER_00]: A man with bits I wouldn't expect would ponder so-and-so and so forth.

93
00:05:55,512 --> 00:05:58,272
[SPEAKER_00]: And same thing for consciousness now.

94
00:05:59,013 --> 00:05:59,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, it's...

95
00:06:00,433 --> 00:06:07,498
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you think this similes to me, just as saying that, yeah, it's, you know, not substantial, this consciousness, like you're saying, form?

96
00:06:08,178 --> 00:06:16,564
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is there more, eh... descending from that simile, like, oh, it's like a deceptive, or it's misleading, or something?

97
00:06:16,724 --> 00:06:17,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, you can, yeah.

98
00:06:18,365 --> 00:06:19,806
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you can say that, basically.

99
00:06:19,826 --> 00:06:26,530
[SPEAKER_01]: It's, eh... You... You have misled yourself with assumptions in regard to consciousness.

100
00:06:27,911 --> 00:06:28,111
[SPEAKER_01]: Eh...

101
00:06:28,311 --> 00:06:28,692
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

102
00:06:29,509 --> 00:06:34,290
[SPEAKER_01]: The magician's performance, if that's the simile, it's real as such.

103
00:06:34,751 --> 00:06:36,531
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a magician's performance.

104
00:06:36,591 --> 00:06:37,571
[SPEAKER_01]: This is what he's done.

105
00:06:38,452 --> 00:06:40,732
[SPEAKER_01]: But your belief upon it, depends on you.

106
00:06:41,813 --> 00:06:56,317
[SPEAKER_01]: And the most basic deception, the most basic misconception, is regarding permanent that which is impermanent, regarding pleasurable that which is suffering, and then taking that thing to be yourself, which you cannot be.

107
00:06:58,138 --> 00:06:59,238
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's like the fundamental

108
00:07:00,727 --> 00:07:08,919
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's... is there no problem in people regarding, oh, this is all illusion?

109
00:07:09,540 --> 00:07:15,268
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, as long as you don't blame things for that illusion, but blame your own attitude towards it.

110
00:07:17,150 --> 00:07:30,196
[SPEAKER_01]: But usually, whenever I've heard, oh, illusion, illusion, it was said in a dismissive sense, whereby, oh, this is all illusion, this is all not real, there is no problem, there is no nibbāna, there is nothing which is already a nibbāna, which is everything and nothing.

111
00:07:30,216 --> 00:07:34,818
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just become having all sorts of use, because it's just blanket denial of everything.

112
00:07:35,498 --> 00:07:37,759
[SPEAKER_01]: But sure, illusion is real as such.

113
00:07:38,700 --> 00:07:43,022
[SPEAKER_01]: So, stop being deluded by that illusion and see through what it is.

114
00:07:43,042 --> 00:07:46,563
[SPEAKER_01]: And what it is, it's impermanent suffering, thus it cannot be taken from yourself.

115
00:07:46,903 --> 00:07:48,984
[SPEAKER_01]: It cannot be taken to belong to you.

116
00:07:49,985 --> 00:07:50,385
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's it.

117
00:07:50,865 --> 00:07:51,485
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all it is.

118
00:07:53,266 --> 00:07:58,368
[SPEAKER_01]: Bubbles will... and the foam will keep arising for as long as there is a river foaming and so on.

119
00:07:58,388 --> 00:08:01,890
[SPEAKER_01]: But when the river drains up, even the aggregates break apart, that's it.

120
00:08:01,990 --> 00:08:04,151
[SPEAKER_01]: It's part of the environment and they will not arise again.

121
00:08:04,651 --> 00:08:07,592
[SPEAKER_01]: Not even those illusory bubbles.

122
00:08:14,807 --> 00:08:20,370
[SPEAKER_00]: Another question I have is that you mentioned of internal-external a lot.

123
00:08:20,890 --> 00:08:25,312
[SPEAKER_00]: I was hoping that we could clarify exactly those terms.

124
00:08:26,072 --> 00:08:36,176
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I know it's kind of a broad question, but in general, you know, the Buddhadharma science says internal-external, both internal-external, like even satipaṭṭhāna and citta.

125
00:08:36,496 --> 00:08:36,776
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

126
00:08:37,076 --> 00:08:38,177
[SPEAKER_00]: But I guess the question

127
00:08:38,882 --> 00:08:42,123
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm trying to figure out, it's like, internal it to what?

128
00:08:43,964 --> 00:08:47,265
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, what is, what defines internal versus external?

129
00:08:48,685 --> 00:08:54,367
[SPEAKER_00]: And is that, if it's, if it's, you know, the first thing that comes to mind is, so, internal is mine, external is not.

130
00:08:54,387 --> 00:08:57,448
[SPEAKER_00]: Then the question is, is it valid to look at things like that?

131
00:08:59,389 --> 00:09:01,730
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

132
00:09:02,270 --> 00:09:08,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Usually, yeah, there is a degree of mine associated with internal dimension.

133
00:09:09,248 --> 00:09:22,320
[SPEAKER_01]: But, again, broadly speaking, because it is a broad question, and internally, externally, sometimes, using not always the same sense, as, as, and I think Nirmala said, basically, internal would be everything that pertains to you.

134
00:09:24,401 --> 00:09:27,044
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, externally, it would be everything that doesn't pertain to you.

135
00:09:29,426 --> 00:09:32,889
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's like the broadest, broadest distinction.

136
00:09:34,610 --> 00:09:36,192
[SPEAKER_01]: But, obviously, fundamentally,

137
00:09:36,949 --> 00:09:43,212
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, for an Arahant, for example, there is this conscious body left and all the name and form external.

138
00:09:46,034 --> 00:09:55,739
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can see that that which used to be his, like this conscious body, it doesn't say, oh, the conscious body is left internally for an Arahant and name and form external.

139
00:09:55,799 --> 00:09:59,681
[SPEAKER_01]: It says, what is left is this conscious body and name and form external.

140
00:10:00,341 --> 00:10:04,283
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, when internal means mine, that is gone.

141
00:10:05,630 --> 00:10:20,667
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now, that which used to be taken as mine is still there, but it's not internal in that personal sense, because more often than not, that which pertains to oneself is what is one's, what he has appropriated.

142
00:10:21,227 --> 00:10:27,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, internal and mine do become almost interchangeable when it comes to the attitude of people, but they don't need to be, obviously.

143
00:10:29,410 --> 00:10:53,246
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when the Buddha says something like, kāyā kāyāṁ paśyati, like, mindful of the body, in terms of, or in the body, however you want to translate it, internally, externally, whatever, you're saying that it's a question of, like, seeing one's present attitude of taking the body as mine, meaning myself, but being mindful of that.

144
00:10:53,406 --> 00:10:58,650
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise... Well, you can... that, that, in sattvipaṭṭhānasutra, basically means, you can, if you take it

145
00:10:59,112 --> 00:11:14,846
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're mindful of the body internally, you're mindful from the point of view of this conscious body here, you can be mindful of the same body externally, if you approach it from the point of view of nāmarūpa, which is external, even if it's looking at your own body.

146
00:11:17,048 --> 00:11:19,710
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you always have two points of view at the same time.

147
00:11:19,890 --> 00:11:25,515
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the, that is the, like, the fundamentals of every human experience, or the experience of the five aggregates.

148
00:11:26,856 --> 00:11:36,945
[SPEAKER_01]: And because you have two points of view, there can't be one sent to them, one owner of them, because they are simultaneously arising independent of each other.

149
00:11:37,365 --> 00:11:39,887
[SPEAKER_01]: This conscious body, name and form externally.

150
00:11:43,010 --> 00:11:48,755
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you are seeing a body in the experience of the name and form externally, that body is external body.

151
00:11:50,691 --> 00:11:58,533
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're attending to the body on the aspect of this conscious body right here, because of which I have this experience as a whole, that is the internal body.

152
00:11:59,173 --> 00:12:05,755
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your same distinction is like internal would be like my sense of what my body is, like the idea of my body.

153
00:12:05,775 --> 00:12:11,617
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, for example, I'm mindful of my body seated, but not because I'm seeing it, but because I know it's seated.

154
00:12:11,637 --> 00:12:12,597
[SPEAKER_01]: That would be the external.

155
00:12:12,857 --> 00:12:13,878
[SPEAKER_00]: That's external, too.

156
00:12:14,318 --> 00:12:15,138
[SPEAKER_00]: That would be external.

157
00:12:15,618 --> 00:12:16,838
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't understand what internal means.

158
00:12:16,858 --> 00:12:17,238
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

159
00:12:17,839 --> 00:12:18,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Internal dimensions

160
00:12:19,621 --> 00:12:23,623
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the dimension whereby, if you obviously attend it externally, it's not the internal dimension.

161
00:12:25,004 --> 00:12:27,166
[SPEAKER_01]: The internal dimension is the conscious body right here.

162
00:12:27,746 --> 00:12:32,529
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of which, you can doubt what the internal dimension right here is.

163
00:12:32,549 --> 00:12:34,310
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because the internal dimension is already there.

164
00:12:35,631 --> 00:12:36,691
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not accessible.

165
00:12:37,212 --> 00:12:40,454
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's accessible only through discernment, so to speak.

166
00:12:41,034 --> 00:12:46,117
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why, if you discern it, you can't appropriate it anymore.

167
00:12:46,677 --> 00:12:49,059
[SPEAKER_01]: You can only appropriate it when you don't know what it is.

168
00:12:50,205 --> 00:12:50,805
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, it's mine.

169
00:12:51,305 --> 00:12:54,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, this vague notion of internal is a notion of mine.

170
00:12:55,507 --> 00:12:59,988
[SPEAKER_01]: When it's discerned, when there is no ignorance in regard to it, there is no mine in regard to it.

171
00:13:00,008 --> 00:13:04,849
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, for an ārahant, it's just this conscious body and name and form externally.

172
00:13:13,651 --> 00:13:15,452
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't get it.

173
00:13:16,072 --> 00:13:17,352
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, no, I know, I know.

174
00:13:17,412 --> 00:13:19,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it the consciousness is constantly flooding inside you?

175
00:13:19,960 --> 00:13:20,560
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't appear.

176
00:13:23,461 --> 00:13:25,601
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I guess, it's the negative.

177
00:13:26,282 --> 00:13:26,882
[SPEAKER_01]: You can say that.

178
00:13:27,962 --> 00:13:34,383
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, it appears as a negative, but yeah, if you make it positive, then it just becomes positive, which is not this, this conscious body.

179
00:13:34,563 --> 00:13:35,804
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's there, it's external.

180
00:13:36,104 --> 00:13:36,784
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

181
00:13:40,045 --> 00:13:49,347
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the external, name and form, is because of that which external name and form has here.

182
00:13:51,283 --> 00:13:55,145
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, sure.

183
00:13:55,185 --> 00:14:00,188
[SPEAKER_00]: You can say that.

184
00:14:03,470 --> 00:14:09,273
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what I mean when I say that, in a way, everything is external to you.

185
00:14:09,894 --> 00:14:11,775
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, nothing can be mine.

186
00:14:14,036 --> 00:14:20,840
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you... if you wanna... if you wanna... if you start... if a person starts practicing this passion, then the result will be

187
00:14:21,503 --> 00:14:28,826
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, what is taken to be internal, as mine, and then the opposing world external, well, those components, so to speak, remain.

188
00:14:28,926 --> 00:14:30,926
[SPEAKER_01]: But what changes is the attitude in regard to them.

189
00:14:30,966 --> 00:14:40,190
[SPEAKER_01]: So now, you really... it becomes more accurate to say there is external, external, and there is the less external, which used to be internal.

190
00:14:42,371 --> 00:14:44,931
[SPEAKER_00]: Does the internal sense of one's body appear?

191
00:14:45,232 --> 00:14:49,253
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, is it... Well, if it appears, it appears on the level of nāmarūpa.

192
00:14:50,124 --> 00:14:51,765
[SPEAKER_01]: And as such, it's external, yeah.

193
00:14:52,285 --> 00:15:01,810
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, for a patujana, when all that is taken as mind, he takes it, like nāmarūpa to be his, and whatever pertains to his body, so it's all a mess, obviously.

194
00:15:02,490 --> 00:15:08,653
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, if it is designated into a phenomenon, positive phenomenon, then it's nāmarūpa, then it is external as such.

195
00:15:09,293 --> 00:15:19,318
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, because I've understood your statement to some degree in the past, where it's like the body that you can't directly access, but that by which there is feeling, consciousness, whatever.

196
00:15:21,001 --> 00:15:26,445
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you cannot access that directly through feeling, consciousness, perception, because it precedes them structurally.

197
00:15:26,645 --> 00:15:26,885
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

198
00:15:27,886 --> 00:15:28,066
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

199
00:15:28,907 --> 00:15:44,339
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can discern that this body, that you do see externally, as a part of nāmarūpa, when it arises and such, is that because of which, because of which presence, right here and now, this whole experience is in the first place?

200
00:15:45,700 --> 00:15:49,383
[SPEAKER_01]: And is that presence, right here and now, it's what this conscious body is?

201
00:15:52,047 --> 00:16:00,632
[SPEAKER_00]: So, perhaps, one could say, then, that an internal sense of one's body is kind of like... like consciousness.

202
00:16:00,732 --> 00:16:01,793
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like negative.

203
00:16:02,694 --> 00:16:03,294
[SPEAKER_01]: It is negative.

204
00:16:03,414 --> 00:16:03,694
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

205
00:16:03,714 --> 00:16:06,116
[SPEAKER_01]: Because this conscious body, it comes in a bundle.

206
00:16:06,176 --> 00:16:06,416
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

207
00:16:06,476 --> 00:16:08,117
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't separate one from the other.

208
00:16:08,157 --> 00:16:12,500
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't conceive, even investigate consciousness apart from the form of body.

209
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:17,463
[SPEAKER_01]: Nor can you even, even, even ponder on the notion of body if there is no consciousness in regard to it.

210
00:16:18,122 --> 00:16:26,306
[SPEAKER_01]: But what happens is, with the appropriation, then, whatever resembles this conscious body, it just ends up being piled up on top of that negative phenomenon.

211
00:16:26,746 --> 00:16:27,927
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, it becomes quite positive.

212
00:16:28,167 --> 00:16:33,190
[SPEAKER_01]: The positive internal of a putujana, which, for him, is mine.

213
00:16:35,471 --> 00:16:43,495
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in that sense, internal... perhaps the designation is always a designation, but it depends on how one regards the designation.

214
00:16:43,515 --> 00:16:44,255
[SPEAKER_00]: That's important.

215
00:16:44,475 --> 00:16:45,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Would you agree?

216
00:16:45,196 --> 00:16:45,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, of course.

217
00:16:45,776 --> 00:16:46,296
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

218
00:16:46,316 --> 00:16:47,557
[SPEAKER_01]: Designation is whenever the problem is.

219
00:16:49,858 --> 00:16:57,102
[SPEAKER_01]: So, people think designation is a problem and they try not saying I, or I am, or I will, because, you know, there is no self.

220
00:16:57,382 --> 00:16:58,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

221
00:16:58,243 --> 00:16:58,743
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

222
00:16:58,803 --> 00:17:00,024
[SPEAKER_01]: And that was happening to the Buddha.

223
00:17:00,064 --> 00:17:05,347
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, no, the Thalitta still uses those words, those terms, but he knows them as mere designations.

224
00:17:05,387 --> 00:17:08,588
[SPEAKER_01]: And for him, that does not stand for the actual sense of self.

225
00:17:10,369 --> 00:17:13,131
[SPEAKER_00]: This question came out yesterday.

226
00:17:13,151 --> 00:17:14,272
[SPEAKER_00]: You were down there, right?

227
00:17:14,292 --> 00:17:14,952
[SPEAKER_00]: That was yesterday?

228
00:17:15,332 --> 00:17:15,833
[SPEAKER_01]: Day before.

229
00:17:15,973 --> 00:17:16,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Day before.

230
00:17:18,175 --> 00:17:19,296
[SPEAKER_00]: You mentioned thoughts.

231
00:17:19,976 --> 00:17:21,537
[SPEAKER_00]: All your thoughts are external.

232
00:17:21,657 --> 00:17:26,660
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why people could potentially, you know, read your thoughts, if they have that power.

233
00:17:26,940 --> 00:17:27,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

234
00:17:27,761 --> 00:17:30,322
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, that got me thinking, like, how could that be?

235
00:17:30,463 --> 00:17:30,683
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

236
00:17:30,703 --> 00:17:41,349
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that means, basically, that thoughts that are internal, for somebody who's developed their mind, become just less external than things that are externally external for everyone.

237
00:17:44,073 --> 00:17:44,754
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

238
00:17:45,454 --> 00:17:48,016
[SPEAKER_01]: Those thoughts don't change from the internal domain where they are.

239
00:17:48,196 --> 00:17:50,838
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't go out into the external where everybody can see them.

240
00:17:51,178 --> 00:17:51,899
[SPEAKER_01]: They stay where they are.

241
00:17:51,919 --> 00:17:54,661
[SPEAKER_01]: But what changes is the mind in regard to these domains.

242
00:17:55,582 --> 00:17:57,423
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you can see other people's thoughts.

243
00:17:58,124 --> 00:18:04,889
[SPEAKER_01]: They are not aware of it, obviously, because for them, it's gripped by the whole pile-up of internal mind dimension, which they just regard

244
00:18:05,430 --> 00:18:12,013
[SPEAKER_01]: It's hidden, but it's actually out there and it can be seen from from the way they would speak, act, behave.

245
00:18:12,633 --> 00:18:20,196
[SPEAKER_01]: All of these thoughts as possibilities become discerned as external to somebody whose mind has been developed with regard to more domains.

246
00:18:20,716 --> 00:18:23,217
[SPEAKER_00]: They're just part of nāma, rūpa and sañña things too.

247
00:18:23,237 --> 00:18:25,018
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, of course.

248
00:18:25,755 --> 00:18:30,378
[SPEAKER_00]: But it seems that the idea of them being internal comes as though they're in my brain.

249
00:18:30,458 --> 00:18:30,878
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

250
00:18:30,938 --> 00:18:32,079
[SPEAKER_00]: They're placed in it.

251
00:18:32,299 --> 00:18:36,702
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly... You look at the foam, the bubble, and there's nothing you can place in it.

252
00:18:38,022 --> 00:18:39,783
[SPEAKER_01]: It has a reason as such, and it will go.

253
00:18:40,444 --> 00:18:43,345
[SPEAKER_01]: Same goes for every single organ, every single part of your body.

254
00:18:43,365 --> 00:18:44,706
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly it.

255
00:18:44,746 --> 00:18:45,987
[SPEAKER_01]: It has no substance.

256
00:18:46,727 --> 00:18:49,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing can be placed in it that is substantial.

257
00:18:52,015 --> 00:19:01,540
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why people need to keep pondering on this, to stop associating any notions of mine as in me, in this brain, in this body, in this heart.

258
00:19:01,560 --> 00:19:02,781
[SPEAKER_01]: Consciousness is in here.

259
00:19:03,821 --> 00:19:03,981
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

260
00:19:04,441 --> 00:19:06,843
[SPEAKER_01]: These things are inaccessible to any such placements.

261
00:19:07,543 --> 00:19:08,503
[SPEAKER_01]: They're mere bubbles.

262
00:19:08,944 --> 00:19:09,864
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing to them.

263
00:19:12,946 --> 00:19:14,166
[SPEAKER_00]: But they're still phenomena.

264
00:19:14,386 --> 00:19:15,567
[SPEAKER_01]: As such, exactly, yeah.

265
00:19:16,267 --> 00:19:17,548
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why people get confused.

266
00:19:17,868 --> 00:19:20,970
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why they can maintain this misconception.

267
00:19:21,628 --> 00:19:26,782
[SPEAKER_01]: Because these things keep arising, and the misconception keeps persisting, because nobody undoes it.

268
00:19:40,089 --> 00:19:47,933
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, the rūpa in name and form, nāmarūpa, that's still external form, because it's name and form with tāngra.

269
00:19:48,013 --> 00:19:49,874
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that internal sense.

270
00:19:50,314 --> 00:19:55,797
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's not that particular rūpa because of which there are sāryasāṃsmādhi statues.

271
00:19:56,017 --> 00:19:56,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

272
00:19:56,957 --> 00:19:59,879
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just the rūpa in the ultimate kind of general sense.

273
00:20:02,080 --> 00:20:09,123
[SPEAKER_01]: But there are certain matter, there is certain matter around the domain of matter.

274
00:20:09,668 --> 00:20:12,991
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of which, there is the arising of sights, sounds, smell.

275
00:20:13,211 --> 00:20:14,772
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your eyes, your tongue, your ears.

276
00:20:15,112 --> 00:20:15,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

277
00:20:15,373 --> 00:20:24,199
[SPEAKER_01]: When, when consciousness is there juxtaposed with that matter, and that whole relationship precedes you entirely, you can't even access it in your thoughts or abstraction.

278
00:20:25,520 --> 00:20:26,481
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't it?

279
00:20:26,581 --> 00:20:30,645
[SPEAKER_01]: Only through like that negative direction of discernment, which is what discernment is.

280
00:20:33,731 --> 00:20:36,433
[SPEAKER_00]: You need the eye working and a form.

281
00:20:36,453 --> 00:20:38,074
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't just have one or the other.

282
00:20:38,234 --> 00:20:39,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

283
00:20:45,678 --> 00:20:53,182
[SPEAKER_00]: You mentioned also in previous talks this idea of consciousness being external, like with the object.

284
00:20:53,202 --> 00:20:55,604
[SPEAKER_01]: With that form.

285
00:20:55,764 --> 00:20:56,844
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

286
00:20:56,904 --> 00:21:03,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Not necessarily with... well, with the material aspect of the object, not with the actual object.

287
00:21:04,542 --> 00:21:09,085
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the object is the result of consciousness and form already being there just the pose.

288
00:21:10,166 --> 00:21:10,466
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

289
00:21:10,486 --> 00:21:14,328
[SPEAKER_00]: My experience of an object is different from the material of an object.

290
00:21:14,368 --> 00:21:15,489
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you can't say that.

291
00:21:16,529 --> 00:21:24,234
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that comparison will imply you somehow accessing the the relationship between consciousness and the object and then inferring that they're different.

292
00:21:24,964 --> 00:21:26,305
[SPEAKER_01]: But saying that it's the same...

293
00:21:26,325 --> 00:21:26,946
[SPEAKER_01]: It's conceivable.

294
00:21:27,346 --> 00:21:27,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

295
00:21:27,726 --> 00:21:35,793
[SPEAKER_01]: Saying it's the same, different, both same and different, neither same or different, makes no sense, because all of those, either of those, would require you accessing that which you can't.

296
00:21:37,294 --> 00:21:44,380
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, people maintaining notions that this is the same, or this is what the matter is, or this is different from matter, or this is in the matter, or matter is in it.

297
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:46,862
[SPEAKER_01]: All of those things are conceivings.

298
00:21:47,022 --> 00:21:47,302
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

299
00:21:47,342 --> 00:21:51,746
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can't have the second part of that relationship for which you would need.

300
00:21:52,656 --> 00:21:55,077
[SPEAKER_01]: to have access in order to make all these conceivings.

301
00:21:56,398 --> 00:21:57,258
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where they're wrong.

302
00:21:57,778 --> 00:22:02,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if, say, miraculously, they correspond to how things are, that doesn't matter.

303
00:22:02,260 --> 00:22:03,481
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can't know.

304
00:22:03,501 --> 00:22:04,961
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't access how things are.

305
00:22:05,621 --> 00:22:09,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you conceiving them to be in a certain way, will always be a misrepresentation.

306
00:22:15,252 --> 00:22:19,936
[SPEAKER_00]: It's interesting to think about the idea of accessibility versus not being accessible.

307
00:22:20,497 --> 00:22:23,079
[SPEAKER_00]: Not something you... Not accessible, but there.

308
00:22:23,379 --> 00:22:23,839
[SPEAKER_00]: But there.

309
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:24,300
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

310
00:22:25,501 --> 00:22:39,153
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't access... you stop trying to conceive the access when the designation there does not move towards it, so to speak, in any direction, does not try to access it, even by implication.

311
00:22:39,793 --> 00:22:41,895
[SPEAKER_01]: When the mind is fully developed in regard to it,

312
00:22:42,508 --> 00:22:47,551
[SPEAKER_01]: Then it stops being a conceiving of that because of which the mind is there in the first place.

313
00:22:48,812 --> 00:22:52,374
[SPEAKER_00]: So, so it's like taking up what's not accessible, trying to...

314
00:22:52,494 --> 00:22:53,995
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, yeah, that's what conceiving is.

315
00:22:54,936 --> 00:22:55,496
[SPEAKER_01]: Taking up.

316
00:22:56,017 --> 00:22:59,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Just, just by leaning too much or leaning a bit away from.

317
00:23:00,339 --> 00:23:00,660
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

318
00:23:00,860 --> 00:23:03,361
[SPEAKER_01]: Or assuming that it's in, out, both in and out.

319
00:23:03,441 --> 00:23:04,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Neither in or out.

320
00:23:04,262 --> 00:23:05,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Assuming that it's onward.

321
00:23:05,803 --> 00:23:07,024
[SPEAKER_01]: Assuming that it's accessible.

322
00:23:08,625 --> 00:23:10,366
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Mūla Pāryāya Sutta just

323
00:23:11,120 --> 00:23:14,622
[SPEAKER_01]: And it goes around all of the different objects.

324
00:23:14,822 --> 00:23:15,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

325
00:23:15,042 --> 00:23:18,244
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not what you are conceiving.

326
00:23:18,304 --> 00:23:20,426
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the actual attitude of conceiving.

327
00:23:21,266 --> 00:23:21,927
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the problem.

328
00:23:21,947 --> 00:23:23,047
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the root of the problem.

329
00:23:24,288 --> 00:23:33,934
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's important then to realize that the object being there comes prior to whatever assumptions you could make about it.

330
00:23:33,954 --> 00:23:37,376
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, structurally prior, not temporarily prior.

331
00:23:38,097 --> 00:23:42,421
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's simultaneously present, but it structurally precedes it.

332
00:23:43,703 --> 00:23:48,047
[SPEAKER_01]: The structure of movement, the notion of movement, is preceded by it.

333
00:23:48,827 --> 00:23:51,570
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't then access it as a result of that.

334
00:23:54,913 --> 00:23:56,054
[SPEAKER_01]: Structural space as well.

